  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nHigh pressure will center itself right over the middle Ohio Valley \nand central Appalachians over the next several days, bringing \ndaytime temperatures in the 70s and 80s with low humidity. Overnight \ntemperatures will run a bit cooler than normal over the next several \ndays, reaching the 50s and lower 60s for lows. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nAn area of low pressure will track through the Ohio Valley from the \nwest Saturday through Sunday, bringing plenty of moisture along with \nit. Southwesterly winds will bring 70 degree dew point air into the \nregion, and precipitable water values will range from 1.80 to 2.20 \ninches. This will lead to widespread rain, and localized pockets of \nheavier rain and flooding will be possible. In addition, if the \natmosphere can destabilize Sunday afternoon, there is a chance of a \nfew strong to severe thunderstorms. Several forecast models project \nMLCAPE values of 800-1,000 J/kg and 0-6 km shear values of 35-40 kts \ndeveloping by Sunday afternoon. The Storm Prediction Center mentions \nthis potential in their Day 4 discussion, but confidence is not high \nenough for them to outline a specific threat at this time.\n\nThere is a lot of forecast model inconsistency regarding the \nsynoptic pattern from Monday onward. A few models show an upper-\nlevel low sitting over the middle-Atlantic states during this \ntimeframe, which would keep showers and thunderstorms in the \narea each day. These trends will be monitored in the coming days.",
